n and this is  connected with with that certainly  lexically and I think even conceptually  then he talks about what we're often  translating as affections here path a  pathos is the singular of that and a  pathos can mean anything that we suffer  but it's also a term that's used for the  emotions because in part the Aristotle  and the other you know Greek writers on  this viewed emotions as something that  you know are sort of imposed upon us  either by your outside you know things  impinging from the firm from our  environment events we also contribute  something to it but but they kind of  grip us and and we don't necessarily  choose them as such the way we seem to  do with her actions now how are these  connected with each other we're gonna  have to disentangle them a bit aerosol  starts out by talking about the the  passive qualities and he very helpfully  begins with several examples sweetness  and bitterness right coldness and warmth  whiteness and blackness those are is go  to colors he's going to discuss some  other colors a little bit later in the  chapter so the things that have these  the things that that in which these  things are like the whiteness or  blackness right or blueness the the tie  is blue because it has blueness in it  remember all that talked about in a  subject predicated of a subject right  it's a blue tie it has a certain quality  as that it could be a red tie it could  be a green time being a tie being blue  you know is not essential to being a tie  but but it's you know the way this tie  is right blue shirt as well so  all of them are called of that quality  we get to about blue things or lukewarm  things or sour things right and that  sourness or blueness or lukewarm  entities apoyo tastes and now Aristotle  wants to make clear that and I think  this probably only holds for most cases  he talks about it in rather sweeping  terms that it's not something that's  changed in the thing per se  for example honey is sweet right but  it's no